The United States has a strong and diverse economy that is the world's largest by nominal GDP. In 2024, the country's GDP was estimated to be $291.85 trillion, a significant increase from the previous year's GDP of $277.21 trillion in 2023. This growth can be attributed to factors such as a strong labor market, low unemployment rates, and an increasing consumer spending due to the relaxation of restrictions from the pandemic.

Key economic indicators such as inflation, interest rates, and employment rates are also important indicators of the country's economic health. Inflation in 2024 was estimated to be around 2%, which is within the Federal Reserve's target range. Interest rates were expected to remain low, with the Federal Reserve projecting a federal funds rate of 1.5% to 2.5%. The unemployment rate in 2024 was projected to be at 3.5%, which is lower than the pre-pandemic level of 3.7% in 2019.

